    ("fusion_set_design_rules", "Load a FAB's design rules (DRC) into the open board. The rule packs live on the wiki page adom/pcb-design-rules (JLCPCB, PCBWay, OSH Park, Seeed, Eurocircuits, Aisler, Elecrow, Advanced Circuits, Sierra, MacroFab, NextPCB, ALLPCB and 3rd party fab; one .edru per vendor/process/layer count, every number citing the vendor's own capabilities page): the bridge fetches the pack, stages it on the box, and the add-in runs `drc load`. {action:'list'} answers with every (fab, process, layers) row so you can offer the user a real choice; no fab means the bundled 3rd party fab rules for the board's layer count (what ab's removed fusion_apply_3rdpartyfab_rules did); druPath loads an .edru already on the Fusion machine; druText/druBase64 loads one that exists only on your side. Layers auto-detects from the open board. ALWAYS confirm with fusion_alerts afterwards: a refused file is an in-canvas toast, never a dialog.",
     {"fab": "optional str vendorSlug (jlcpcb|pcbway|osh-park|seeed-fusion|eurocircuits|aisler|elecrow|advanced-circuits-4pcb|sierra-circuits|macrofab|nextpcb|allpcb|adom-3rdpartyfab)", "process": "optional str (the vendor's tier: standard|advanced|proto|enig|hasl|...; default standard)", "layers": "optional str (auto|2|4|6)", "druPath": "optional str .edru/.dru ALREADY on the Fusion machine, loaded as-is", "druText": "optional str: the whole .edru/.dru file as text, for rules that exist only on YOUR side (a container). The bridge stages it on the box and loads it (1.10.37; replaces ab's removed fusion_load_design_rules)", "druBase64": "optional str: the same, base64-encoded", "druName": "optional str: file name for the staged copy", "action": "optional str (apply|list|export|show)"}, {"activeWorkspace": "str", "description": "str", "pack": "dict {fab, vendor, process, layers, file, record}"}, 90, None, False, {"fab": "jlcpcb", "layers": "2"}),
